meta { title = "Kalandra documentation", description = "Learn native CPU and GPU programming with Kalandra.", order = 1, group = "Start here" }

Kalandra is a native language for CPU and GPU computing. Write typed functions, compose numerical operations and choose where tasks run. CPU workers and Apple Metal tasks share structured parallel scopes.

h2 { "Your first program" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  6 * 7
""" }

Put this in main.k. The native compiler produces an executable; running it displays the value returned by main.

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra-check main.k
kalandra main.k -o program
./program
""" }

Unless a page gives a project layout, save each complete language example as main.k and use the check, compile and run commands above. Examples on the GPU pages use their Metal commands instead. Export-only modules are companion files for the application shown beside them; the troubleshooting page labels its intentionally rejected program explicitly.

h2 { "Choose a path" }

